tel 1.0

base contingency
import soundness

info   error handling as a choice at the call site: `raises` in the type, a `Tactic` in scope
detail
    _Contingency_ separates the code which can fail from the decision of what a failure does.
    A method which can fail says so in its return type with `raises`; inside it, `abort`
    reports an error and stops, and `raise` reports one and continues. The caller decides what
    becomes of the error by putting a **tactic** — a `Tactic[error]` — in scope: throw it, make
    the result absent, collect it with others, replace it with a value, translate it into
    another error, or terminate the process. The fallible method never says which, so one body
    serves every response.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
    extends Error(m"the port $port is not available")

    case class Connection(port: Int)

    def connect(port: Int): Connection raises PortError =
      if port > 1024 then Connection(port) else abort(PortError(port))
    ```

    `Connection raises PortError` is only an alias for the context function type
    `Tactic[PortError] ?=> Connection`, so a `raises` method is a method taking its
    error-handling policy as a contextual argument, and `abort` is what calls into it. The
    requirement propagates: a caller of `connect` either declares `raises PortError` itself or
    discharges the obligation with one of the handlers below. The same failing call — decoding
    text which is not a number, which raises `Number.Error` — under each:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label Thrown, under the ambient strategy
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          locally:
            import strategies.throwUnsafely
            t"abc".as[Int]                          // throws Number.Error
    frame
      label Absent, under `safely`
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          safely(t"abc".as[Int])                    // Unset
    frame
      label A value either way, under `attempt`
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          attempt[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Int])     // Attempt.Failure(Number.Error(…))
    frame
      label The error itself, under `capture`
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          capture[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Int])     // Number.Error(t"abc", Int, Unparseable)
    frame
      label Replaced, under `recover`
      code
          import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

          recover:
            case Number.Error(text, _, _) => 0
          . protect(t"abc".as[Int])                 // 0
    ##
    ```

    The pieces, by the question each answers:

    - **Declaring failure**: `raises`, `raise`, `abort`, and `Tactic`/`Emit`, the capabilities
      behind them. An `Emit` can only report; a `Tactic` can also abort.
    - **Choosing once, broadly**: the `strategies` package, whose givens are imported by name —
      `throwUnsafely` for prototypes and entry points, `throwSafely` under `CanThrow`,
      `uncheckedErrors` and `fatalErrors` for errors classified `Unchecked` or `Fatal`, and
      `mitigation` for errors with a `Mitigable` translation. `import soundness.*` brings none
      of them in, because a wildcard import never imports givens.
    - **Choosing locally, for a block**: `safely` (absent on failure), `unsafely` (throw, and
      say so), `throwErrors`, `attempt` (an `Attempt`), `capture` (the error, for tests),
      `amalgamate` (a union), and the handler builders `recover`, `mitigate`, `accrue` and
      `handle`, each written as match cases and applied to a block with `protect`.
    - **Collecting every failure**: `raise` records and continues, so an accruing tactic —
      `accrue`, `track`, `validate` — gathers every error of a validation pass; `Foci`,
      `focus` and `Pointer` say *where* each one happened; `venture` and `guard` keep a
      recorded failure from cascading into spurious ones; `Tactic#tolerate` discards a block's
      failure within the ambient tactic.
    - **Classifying errors**: `Unchecked` (may be thrown unhandled), `Fatal` (ends the
      process, with an `Exit` status) and `Mitigable` (translates into another error).
    - **Errors as values**: `Attempt`, `Venture`, `Errors`, `Validation`, `Expectation.Error`.

    A tactic is a capability. `Emit`, and so every `Tactic`, extends `caps.Control`: it
    captures a return point on the stack of the code which installed it, so a body which runs
    on another thread (a task's) cannot retain one, and a tactic is never used after its scope
    has closed. The ambient strategies are the exception, and deliberately pure: an unscoped
    throw retains no return point. Every error is a `Hazard` — a pure `Exception` — so no error
    can smuggle a capability out of the scope which confined it; a `Diagnostics` given (from
    `errorDiagnostics`) decides whether each error captures a stack trace, and the tactics
    which discard their errors supply `Diagnostics.omit` themselves.

    The library is one source set, `contingency.core`, built on `fulminate` (for `Error`,
    `Message` and `Diagnostics`) and `vacuous` (for `Optional`), and it compiles for the JVM,
    Scala.js and Scala Native alike. Everything but `raising` and `FatalTactic` is exported to
    the `soundness` umbrella, and `strategies` merges with the umbrella's own package of that
    name.

refer contingency.Tactic
refer contingency.raises
refer contingency.strategies
refer contingency.safely
refer contingency.recover
refer contingency.accrue
refer fulminate.Error

entry .Tactic   the capability to report an error and to abort with one: what `raises` asks for
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` is an `Emit[error]` which can additionally `abort`: a value-replacing
      abnormal exit. `success raises error` is `Tactic[error]^ ?=> success`, so every fallible
      method takes a tactic as a contextual parameter, `raise` calls its `record` and `abort`
      calls its `abort`. What each does is the tactic's choice: a `ThrowTactic` throws, an
      `OptionalTactic` breaks out of `safely` with `Unset`, an `AccrueTactic` folds the error
      into an accumulation and carries on.

      The type is contravariant in `error`, which is what lets the ambient
      `ThrowTactic[Hazard]` satisfy a `Tactic[PortError]`. A tactic is rarely built by hand:
      the handlers (`safely`, `attempt`, `recover`…) and the `strategies` givens supply one,
      and `summon[Tactic[PortError]]` reaches the one in scope, as a method body does when it
      calls `#tolerate`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def lenient(text: Text): Optional[Int] raises Number.Error =
        summon[Tactic[Number.Error]].tolerate(text.as[Int])
      ```

      A tactic is a `caps.Control` capability, shared rather than exclusive: one tactic is
      legitimately aliased by everything which reports into its scope (a codec summoned for
      `as` captures the same tactic `as` takes as evidence), and raising is not a consuming
      use. The boundary-based tactics close over a stack label, so a tactic cannot be retained
      by a body which runs on another thread, and must not be used after the block which
      installed it has returned.
  refer contingency.Emit
  refer contingency.raises
  refer contingency.strategies
  entry #abort   reports an error and does not return
      The value-replacing exit. Under a fail-fast tactic the error escapes at once; under an
      accruing tactic it is folded in and the enclosing aggregation scope is surrendered; under
      a `VentureTactic` only the venture is abandoned.
  entry #certify   the flush point of an aggregation scope: surrenders it if anything was recorded
      If the tactic is `#tainted`, `certify` does not return — the scope escapes with its
      accumulated errors; otherwise it is a no-op. On every fail-fast tactic it is always a
      no-op, since a recorded error would already have escaped. `guard` calls it to skip a
      block under a tainted tactic, and the package-level `certify[error]()` calls it on the
      tactic in scope.
  entry #tainted   whether any error has been recorded in this tactic's aggregation scope
      Truthfully `false` on every fail-fast tactic, where `record` never returns, and forwarded
      through `#contramap`, so taint crosses a `mitigate`d library boundary: a `guard` inside a
      library is skipped when the caller's scope is already tainted.
  entry #tolerate   runs a block and discards whatever it raises: its value, or `Unset`
      The block's result, or `Unset` once an error has been recorded or the block aborts —
      within the ambient tactic, not under a new one as `safely` would install. The default
      catches the escape a fail-fast tactic makes; an accruing tactic (`AccrueTactic`,
      `TrackTactic`, `VentureTactic`) also rolls its accrual back to what it was before the
      block, so a tolerated fault leaves no trace. This is the lever for a codec decoding an
      optional field leniently: the field's decoder was bound to the ambient tactic when it was
      resolved, so the tactic is the only place its fault can be intercepted.
  entry #contramap   a tactic for another error type, by translating each error into this one
      `tactic.contramap(lambda)` is a `Tactic[error2]^{tactic}` whose `record`, `abort`,
      `certify`, `tainted` and `tolerate` all forward to `tactic`, with each error passed
      through `lambda` first. The lambda is a pure `->` function: a shared capability may
      retain only shared capabilities and pure values, and an error transformer has no
      business capturing anything else. `strategies.mitigation` and `mitigate` are built on
      it.

entry .Emit   the capability to report an error as a side-effect, without the power to abort
  detail
      An `Emit[error]` can `record` an error but cannot, of itself, stop: whether control
      continues afterwards is the implementation's choice. `raise` needs only an `Emit`;
      `abort` needs a full `Tactic`. So an `Emit[error] ?=>` obligation is weaker than
      `raises error`, and a `Tactic` in scope discharges either. `handle` is the construct
      which supplies bare emitters: each case of its handler becomes an `Emit` whose `record`
      runs the case body in place, and the protected block carries on.

      An emitter is a `caps.Control` capability, and is `Findable`, so a missing one is
      reported with the choice packages which would supply it. It may retain only other
      `Control` capabilities: a handler lambda passed to `.apply` can raise into an outer
      tactic, but cannot capture a logger or a stream.
  refer contingency.Tactic
  refer contingency.handle
  entry .apply   an emitter which runs a handler at the emit point
      `Emit(handler)` (with a `Diagnostics` in scope) is an `Emit[error]^` whose `record`
      calls `handler` on the error and returns. The handler is a `consume`d function which may
      capture only `caps.Control` capabilities — an outer tactic it raises into — since the
      emitter it becomes is itself a classified capability. This is what `handle { … }.protect`
      installs for each covered error type.
  entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` errors reported through this emitter are built with
      Every `record` and `abort` takes its error as a `Diagnostics ?=> error` thunk and
      evaluates it with this, so the tactic — not the raising code — decides whether a stack
      trace is captured. `ThrowTactic` captures one; `OptionalTactic` omits it, since the error
      is discarded.
  entry #record   reports an error; whether control continues is the implementation's choice
  entry #contramap   an emitter for another error type, by translating each error into this one
      `emit.contramap(lambda)` is an `Emit[error2]^{emit}` forwarding every record through the
      pure `lambda`.

entry .strategies   the broad, importable tactics: throw, throw under `CanThrow`, unchecked, fatal, mitigate
  detail
      A *strategy* is a tactic imported once and applied broadly, where a *tactic* installed
      by `safely` or `recover` is local to a block; both are the one type `Tactic`. The choice
      is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these givens in, and one is
      imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      t"abc".as[Int]   // throws Number.Error
      ```

      The ambient strategies are for a program's entry points and for prototyping — the
      intended progression is to prototype under `throwUnsafely` and convert the places which
      matter to real handling as the program hardens, with no change to the fallible code. A
      method-local `import strategies.throwUnsafely` shadows the method's own `raises` tactic
      for every `abort` below it, which is the nesting rule working as designed, but easy to
      do by accident inside a `raises` method.

      `throwUnsafely`, `uncheckedErrors` and `fatalErrors` are pure values rather than tracked
      capabilities: an unscoped throw (or a termination in place) retains no return point, and
      a tracked ambient tactic would either fail the level check inside a derived codec's
      synthesised thunk or be captured by every lambda raising through it. `throwSafely` is
      the scoped one, capturing the `CanThrow` evidence it throws under.
  refer contingency.ThrowTactic
  refer contingency.Unchecked
  refer contingency.Fatal
  refer contingency.Mitigable
  entry .throwUnsafely   throws every error as an ordinary exception, with no capability required
      A pure `ThrowTactic[Hazard]^{}`, so by contravariance it satisfies every `Tactic[error]`
      at once. The error is built with `Diagnostics.capture`, so it carries a stack trace.
      Suits scripts, prototypes and the outermost layer of a program; a library should say
      what it does instead, with `unsafely` and a reason beside it where an obligation cannot
      fire.
  entry .throwSafely   throws, but only where a `CanThrow[error]` capability is in scope
      `throwSafely[error]` is a `ThrowTactic[error]^{canThrow}`: it throws exactly as
      `throwUnsafely` does, but it needs an (erased) `CanThrow[error]` given, so the
      possibility of the throw stays visible in the types under `saferExceptions`. Its capture
      set is the `CanThrow` evidence, a `caps.Control` capability, which keeps it out of a
      body which runs on another thread.
  entry .mitigation   a tactic for any error which is `Mitigable to` an error already handled
      Given a `Tactic[error]^` in scope and an `error2 is Mitigable to error`, provides a
      `Tactic[error2]^` which translates each `error2` with `mitigate` and reports it to the
      outer tactic. A low-level failure thus reaches an API boundary already translated into
      that boundary's vocabulary, without every call site restating the translation.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import strategies.mitigation

      case class InputError(text: Text)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"the input $text is not acceptable")

      given Number.Error is Mitigable to InputError = error => InputError(error.text)

      def age(text: Text): Int raises InputError = text.as[Int]
      ```
  entry .fatalErrors   terminates the process with the error's `Exit` status, for errors marked `Fatal`
      A pure `FatalTactic[exception]^{}` for any error type with an `is Fatal` given, which
      says what status to exit with. Suits failures during initialisation, where there is no
      meaningful way to continue.
  entry .uncheckedErrors   throws, unhandled, any error which is marked `Unchecked`
      A pure `UncheckedTactic[error]^{}` for any error with an erased `error is Unchecked`
      given. The error is thrown with a stack trace (`Diagnostics.capture`). It lets a type
      whose errors are not worth handling — an impossible-in-practice failure — be raised
      without a handler at each site, while still being declared.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.uncheckedErrors

      erased given Number.Error is Unchecked = new Unchecked { type Self = Number.Error }

      t"abc".as[Int]   // throws Number.Error, with no other tactic in scope
      ```

entry .raises   `success raises error`: the return type of a method which may raise `error`
    An infix type alias: `success raises error` is `Tactic[error]^ ?=> success`, a context
    function taking the error-handling tactic. A method declared with it is a method taking
    the policy as a contextual argument; `abort` and `raise` inside it call that tactic. A
    method which can fail in more than one way writes each: `Int raises Io.Error raises
    Number.Error`. Calling such a method needs a `Tactic` for each error in scope — a
    `strategies` given, or an enclosing `safely`, `recover`, `attempt`… block — or an
    enclosing method which itself declares `raises` for it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def half(text: Text): Int raises Number.Error = text.as[Int] / 2

    safely(half(t"42"))   // 21
    safely(half(t"x"))    // Unset
    ```

    A `val` initialised by a `raises` call gets the result type, not the context function
    type: the tactic is resolved at the call. A typeclass instance whose method result is `S
    raises E` cannot be written under capture checking (each `?=>` mints a fresh root); such
    an instance takes its `Tactic[E]` as a `using` parameter instead.

entry .raising   `success raising (E1, E2)`: a `raises` for each error in a tuple, as a match type
    `result raising (Io.Error, Number.Error)` reduces to `Tactic[Io.Error] ?=>
    Tactic[Number.Error] ?=> result`, and a non-tuple `raising E` to `Tactic[E] ?=> result`.
    It is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella and cannot yet replace `raises`: a match
    type reducing to a context function in a method's return type crashes the compiler at a
    cross-module use site, so `raises` chained once per error is the form to write.

entry .mitigates   `error mitigates error2`: evidence that `error2` can be translated into `error`
    An infix alias for `error2 is Mitigable to error`, so a context bound or `using` clause
    can be written in the direction the translation reads. See `Mitigable` and
    `strategies.mitigation`.

entry .tracks   `result tracks focus`: a result computed with a `Foci[focus]` in scope
    An infix alias for `Foci[focus] ?=> result`, written after `raises`: `Unit raises
    Json.Error tracks JsonPointer` is a validation which records each error against the
    position it happened at. A `Foci` given is always resolvable (`Foci.default` discards
    everything), so a `tracks` method can be called anywhere, and only an enclosing `track`
    or `validate` makes the positions observable.

entry .raise   reports an error and continues: the value-preserving report
    `raise(error)` calls `record` on the `Emit[error]^` in scope and returns `Unit`. Under a
    fail-fast tactic (`throwUnsafely`, `safely`, `attempt`…) control never comes back; under an
    accruing tactic (`accrue`, `track`, `validate`, `handle`) it does, so the code carries on,
    and the result of the enclosing scope is eventually discarded in favour of the
    accumulated errors. The error is a `Diagnostics ?=> error` thunk, built with the tactic's
    own `Diagnostics`, so a raising method needs no `Diagnostics` of its own.

    Since `raise` returns `Unit`, a method which must continue with a value writes an *ersatz*
    value after it: `raise(error) yet 0`. An ersatz value should be inconsequential — nothing
    downstream should depend on it, and it should not provoke further errors — which is what
    `venture` and `guard` make structural rather than hoped for.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    def lenientAge(text: Text): Int raises Number.Error =
      safely(text.as[Int]).or:
        raise(Number.Error(text, Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable)) yet 0
    ```

    `raise` asks only for an `Emit`, the weaker capability; `abort` needs a `Tactic`.

entry .abort   reports an error and does not return: the value-replacing exit
    `abort(error)` calls `abort` on the `Tactic[error]^` in scope and has type `Nothing`, so
    it can stand anywhere a value is needed. Under a fail-fast tactic the error escapes the
    tactic's scope; under an accruing one the error is folded in and the scope surrendered;
    inside a `venture` only the venture is abandoned. The error thunk is built with the
    tactic's `Diagnostics`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    case class PortError(port: Int)(using Diagnostics)
    extends Error(m"the port $port is not available")

    def connect(port: Int): Int raises PortError =
      if port > 1024 then port else abort(PortError(port))
    ```

entry .certify   surrenders the enclosing aggregation scope if any error has been recorded
    `certify[error]()` calls `Tactic#certify` on the tactic in scope: a no-op under a
    fail-fast tactic, and under an accruing one the point at which a tainted scope escapes
    with its accumulated errors rather than continuing. `guard` is the usual way to express
    the same thing around a block.

entry .safely   runs a block and makes its failure absent: the value, or `Unset`
    `safely(block)` evaluates the block with an `OptionalTactic` in scope and returns
    `Optional[success]`: the block's value, or `Unset` on the first error recorded or aborted.
    The error itself is discarded, so it is built with `Diagnostics.omit` — no stack trace is
    captured — and `safely` needs no `Diagnostics` of its own. An exception thrown by the
    block (the block also receives a `CanThrow[Exception]`) is likewise caught and becomes
    `Unset`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    safely(t"42".as[Int])            // 42
    safely(t"abc".as[Int])           // Unset
    safely(t"abc".as[Int]).or(0)     // 0
    ```

    The error type is inferred from the block's requirement; `safely[Number.Error](…)` names
    it when inference needs help, or when the block's only obligation is the one being
    discharged. `safely` installs a *new* tactic: inside an accruing scope, an error recorded
    in the block is not folded into the outer accrual. `Tactic#tolerate` is the alternative
    which stays inside the ambient tactic.

entry .unsafely   runs a block whose error is asserted not to happen: the value, or a throw
    `unsafely(block)` evaluates the block under a `ThrowTactic`, so an error which does occur
    is thrown as an ordinary, unchecked exception, with a stack trace. It discharges the
    obligation without handling it — legitimate at a program's boundary, in a test which has
    already established the value is fine, or beside a check which has just proved the error
    impossible — and is a searchable marker of the places where the guarantee stops. The block
    also receives an `Unsafe` token, so a method gated `(using Unsafe)` may be called within
    it.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    unsafely(t"42".as[Int])   // 42
    unsafely(t"abc".as[Int])  // throws Number.Error
    ```

    Prefer a total API carrying its proof in the type, then `mitigate` into a named error,
    then `unsafely` with the reason in a comment. A `success` type which is a refined path
    type is widened by `unsafely`'s type variable; put the scoped check inside the API rather
    than around the call.

entry .throwErrors   runs a block under a `ThrowTactic`, given a `CanThrow[error]`
    `throwErrors[error](block)` supplies a `ThrowTactic[error]` to the block, so an error is
    thrown; unlike `unsafely`, it requires a `CanThrow[error]` capability in scope, so the
    throw remains checked under `saferExceptions`. It is the block form of
    `strategies.throwSafely`.

entry .capture   runs a block which is expected to fail, and returns the error it raised
    `capture[error](block)` evaluates the block with an `EitherTactic` and returns the
    `error` it recorded or aborted with. A block which succeeds is itself a failure — the
    test was checking the wrong thing — and aborts with `Expectation.Error` carrying the value
    produced, so `capture` needs a `Tactic[Expectation.Error[success]]^` in scope (the
    ambient `strategies.throwUnsafely` serves) and a `Diagnostics`. An exception of the
    captured type thrown rather than raised is captured too; any other exception propagates.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    capture[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Int]).reason   // Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable
    ```

    The error type must be given explicitly (`ClassTag` is needed to catch it), which is also
    what makes a `capture` in a test read as its assertion: the block must fail with exactly
    this.

entry .attempt   runs a block and returns an `Attempt`: its value, or the error it failed with
    `attempt[error](block)` evaluates the block with an `AttemptTactic` and returns
    `Attempt.Success(value)` or `Attempt.Failure(error)` on the first error recorded or
    aborted, for code which must branch on both outcomes. It needs a `Diagnostics` in scope,
    since the error is kept.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    attempt[Number.Error](t"42".as[Int])    // Attempt.Success(42)
    attempt[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Int]) match
      case Attempt.Failure(error) => error.text   // t"abc"
      case Attempt.Success(value) => t""
    ```

entry .amalgamate   runs a block and returns its value or its error, as a union
    `amalgamate[error](block)` evaluates the block with an `AmalgamateTactic` and returns
    `success | error`: the value, or the first error recorded or aborted. A match on the result
    separates them, with no wrapper. Needs a `Diagnostics`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    amalgamate[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Int]) match
      case error: Number.Error => t"not a number"
      case value: Int          => t"$value"
    ```

entry .abortive   runs a macro's block under a `HaltTactic`: an error becomes a compile error
    For macro implementations, with `Quotes` in scope. `abortive[error](block)` supplies a
    `HaltTactic[error, success]` and `Diagnostics.omit`, so an error recorded or aborted in
    the block halts the compilation with the error's `labelled` message (its `SN-` code and
    text) as the diagnostic. A macro which parses a literal at expansion time wraps the parse
    in `abortive` to report a malformed literal where it is written.

entry .Attempt   the outcome of `attempt`: a success carrying a value, or a failure carrying an error
  detail
      An enum with two cases, `Success(value: result)` and `Failure(value: error)`, where
      `error <: Hazard`. `attempt[error](block)` is how one is produced, and the methods on it
      let the outcome be inspected, transformed, or turned back into a raise.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val outcome: Attempt[Int, Number.Error] = attempt[Number.Error](t"abc".as[Int])

      outcome.success                   // false
      outcome.map(_ * 2)                // Attempt.Failure(…)
      outcome.recover { case _: Number.Error => 0 }   // 0
      ```
  refer contingency.attempt
  entry #failure   whether this is a `Failure`
  entry #success   whether this is a `Success`
  entry #map   transforms the value of a `Success`; a `Failure` passes through unchanged
  entry #handle   replaces the error of a `Failure` with another, by a partial function
      `attempt.handle { case Number.Error(text, _, _) => InputError(text) }` applies the cases
      to a `Failure`'s error, leaving one the cases do not cover as it was, and widens the
      error type to `Hazard`. A `Success` is unchanged.
  entry #acknowledge   runs a side-effect on a `Failure`'s error, and returns this attempt
      For logging a failure without consuming it: the partial function is applied if it is
      defined at the error, and the attempt is returned unchanged either way.
  entry #apply   the value of a `Success`, or aborts with a `Failure`'s error
      `attempt()` is `result raises error`: it turns an `Attempt` back into a raise against
      the tactic in scope, so a value which was captured for inspection can be rejoined to the
      ordinary flow.
  entry #recover   the value of a `Success`, or a replacement computed from a `Failure`'s error
      `attempt.recover { case error => fallback }` is the value if there is one, else the
      partial function applied to the error; it must cover every error it may meet.

entry .recover   handles chosen errors by supplying a replacement value, with `protect`
    `recover { cases }.protect(block)` runs the block with a tactic for each error type the
    cases cover; on the first covered error, the block is abandoned and the matching case
    body's value is returned in its place. The handler is written as match cases, and must be
    written inline — the macro reads the cases at compile time to find which error types they
    cover, which is how `protect` knows which tactics to inject. An error the cases do not
    cover is not handled: its `raises` obligation propagates outward as usual.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    recover:
      case Number.Error(text, _, _) => 0
    . protect(t"abc".as[Int])   // 0
    ```

    A case matching a union of error types (`case error: (Io.Error | Number.Error)`), or an
    alias of a union, registers a handler for each member. A pattern must be exhaustive for
    its error type (a case class pattern with every field a wildcard or binding; a varargs
    field as `arguments*`), or it is a compile error: a `recover` cannot half-handle a type. A
    `recover` written directly inside an `inline def` does not resolve its tactics; delegate
    the body to a non-inline helper method. A case body may raise into an outer tactic, but
    may not capture a non-`Control` capability such as a logger.

entry .Recovery   a `recover` handler, waiting for the block it protects
  detail
      `recover { cases }` is a `Recovery[lambda]` whose type parameter records, as a type
      lambda `[result] =>> (Tactic[E1], …, Tactic[En]) ?=> result`, the error types the cases
      cover; `.protect(block)` then injects one tactic per covered type into the block. The
      value is only ever used as `recover { … }.protect { … }`.
  refer contingency.recover
  entry .protect   runs a block under the recovery's tactics, replacing a covered error with its case's value
      Runs the block inside a `boundary`: each injected tactic, on `record` or `abort`, applies
      the handler to the error and breaks out with the case body's value as the block's
      result. The replacement value travels as a `Recovery.Escape`, so a case body of the
      wrong type is a compile error at the `protect`.
  entry .Escape   the carrier of a recovered value out of a protected block
    detail
        A stackless exception, pure by construction, carrying the case body's value as an
        untyped reference between two frames of one `protect` call. Not raised by user code.
    entry .apply   wraps a value for escape
    entry #value   the recovered value, at its original type
    entry #fillInStackTrace   returns this: an escape never records a stack trace
  entry .EscapeTactic   the tactic behind `protect`, breaking out of a `boundary` with the escaped value
      A `Tactic[Recovery.Escape[result]]` over a `boundary.Label[result]`, with
      `Diagnostics.omit`: its `record` and `abort` unwrap the escape's value and break.
      `protect` contramaps it once per covered error type.
  entry #handler   the match cases, as a partial function from `Exception`

entry .mitigate   translates chosen errors into another error, which then propagates, with `protect`
    `mitigate { cases }.protect(block)` runs the block with a tactic for each error type the
    cases cover; a covered error is passed through its case, and the error the case returns
    is raised against the tactic *in scope for that type* — so the block's low-level failure
    becomes the error the enclosing method declares. Each case body's error type must have a
    `Tactic` in scope at the `mitigate`, or the macro halts with _There is no available
    handler for E_. The cases must be written inline, as for `recover`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    case class InputError(text: Text)(using Diagnostics)
    extends Error(m"the input $text is not acceptable")

    def age(text: Text): Int raises InputError =
      mitigate:
        case Number.Error(text, _, _) => InputError(text)
      . protect(text.as[Int])

    capture[InputError](age(t"abc")).text   // t"abc"
    ```

    Mitigations chain: a `mitigate` inside another turns A into B and then B into C. Taint
    crosses a mitigation — the inner tactic's `tainted` forwards to the outer — so a `guard`
    inside the protected block sees the caller's accrual. A case body builds its error
    outside any `Diagnostics` of the block's, so either import one (`errorDiagnostics`) or
    reuse the original's: `InputError(text)(using error.diagnostics)`. Like `recover`, a
    `mitigate` directly inside an `inline def` finds no tactic; delegate to a non-inline
    method. `strategies.mitigation` applies the same translation implicitly, from a
    `Mitigable` instance, wherever an outer tactic exists.

entry .Mitigation   a `mitigate` handler, waiting for the block it protects
  detail
      `mitigate { cases }` is a `Mitigation[lambda]` recording the covered error types in its
      type lambda; `.protect(block)` injects, for each, the tactic in scope for the case's
      *result* error type, contramapped through the case. Used only as `mitigate { … }.protect
      { … }`.
  refer contingency.mitigate
  entry .protect   runs a block, raising each covered error as its case's translation
  entry #handler   the match cases, as a partial function from `Exception`

entry .accrue   collects every chosen error into one accumulated error, with `protect`
    `accrue(initial)(combine) { cases }.protect(block)` runs the block with an
    `AccrueTactic` for each error type the cases cover. Each covered error the block raises is
    folded into the accumulation with `combine`, and the block continues; when it finishes
    (or aborts), if anything accrued, the accumulated error is raised against the enclosing
    `Tactic[accrual]`, and the block's own result is discarded. If nothing accrued, the
    block's value is returned. The `cases` only *name* the covered types (their bodies are
    not run); `combine` does the folding, and is a pure `->` function, since it may be
    re-run under contention.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import dysasymptotics.linearSize

    case class Invalid(problems: List[Text])(using Diagnostics)
    extends Error(m"${problems.size} problems")

    def combine(all: Invalid, error: Exception): Invalid = error match
      case error: Number.Error => Invalid(all.problems :+ error.text)
      case _                   => all

    capture[Invalid]:
      accrue(Invalid(Nil))(combine) { case _: Number.Error => () }
      . protect:
          raise(Number.Error(t"a", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
          raise(Number.Error(t"b", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
    . problems   // List(t"a", t"b")
    ```

    The accrual type must be a `Hazard`, because it is raised; `validate` is the twin whose
    accumulation is returned as a plain value instead. `track` adds a focus to each error.

entry .Accrual   an `accrue` handler, and the tactic it installs
  detail
      `accrue(initial)(combine) { cases }` is an `Accrual[accrual, lambda]` holding the
      initial accumulation, the combining function and the cases; `.protect(block)` runs the
      block under one `AccrueTactic` shared by every covered error type, and needs a
      `Tactic[accrual]^` and a `Diagnostics` in scope.
  refer contingency.accrue
  entry .protect   runs a block, folding each covered error into the accrual; raises the accrual if any
      Returns the block's value if nothing accrued. Otherwise — whether the block completed,
      aborted, or threw — records the accumulated error against the enclosing
      `Tactic[accrual]` and throws it, so the block's result never reaches the caller.
  entry .AccrueTactic   the tactic `accrue` injects: folds each error into an accumulation and continues
    detail
        A `Tactic[error]` over an initial `accrual` and a pure `combine: (accrual, Exception)
        -> accrual`, with the `Diagnostics` it builds errors with. `record` folds the error in
        and returns; `abort` folds it in and throws the accumulation; `certify` throws the
        accumulation if anything has accrued; `tolerate` rolls the accumulation back to the
        snapshot taken before its block. The accumulation is held in an atomic cell, so the
        tactic is safe to share across the threads of one scope, but `combine` may be re-run
        under contention and must be pure.
    entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` errors are built with
    entry #record   folds an error into the accumulation and returns
    entry #abort   folds an error in and throws the accumulated error
    entry #certify   throws the accumulated error if anything has accrued
    entry #tainted   whether the accumulation differs from the initial value
    entry #tolerate   a block's value, or `Unset` with the accumulation restored to what it was
    entry #accumulated   the accumulation so far
    entry #changed   whether the accumulation differs from the initial value
  entry #handler   the match cases naming the covered error types
  entry #initial   the accumulation before any error
  entry #combine   the pure function folding each error into the accumulation

entry .handle   runs chosen errors' case bodies as side-effects and continues, with `protect`
    `handle { cases }.protect(block)` runs the block with an `Emit` — not a full `Tactic` —
    for each error type the cases cover: a `raise` of a covered error runs its case body in
    place, and control returns to the block, which continues. The case bodies return `Unit`.
    An uncovered error, or one a case body re-raises, stays an obligation of the enclosing
    scope. Since only an `Emit` is injected, an `abort` of a covered error inside the block
    is not discharged by `handle`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    var seen = 0

    handle:
      case Number.Error(text, _, _) => seen += 1
    . protect:
        raise(Number.Error(t"a", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
        raise(Number.Error(t"b", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))

    seen   // 2
    ```

    A case body becomes a `caps.Control` capability's handler, so it may raise into an outer
    tactic but may not capture a non-control capability such as a logger: move such work out
    of the case body.

entry .Handler   a `handle` handler, waiting for the block it protects
  detail
      `handle { cases }` is a `Handler[lambda]` recording the covered error types as a type
      lambda over `Emit`s; `.protect(block)` injects one `Emit` per type, each backed by the
      case body, and needs a `Diagnostics` in scope. Used only as `handle { … }.protect { … }`.
  refer contingency.handle
  entry .protect   runs a block with an `Emit` per covered error, each running its case body in place
  entry #handler   the match cases, as a partial function from `Exception` to `Unit`

entry .track   collects chosen errors, each with the focus it was raised at, into one accumulated error
    `track[focus](initial) { cases }.protect(block)` is `accrue` with positions. The block
    runs with a `Foci[focus]` and a `TrackTactic` for each error type the cases cover; every
    covered error is registered against the foci, together with the focus current at the
    time (set by `focus`), and the block continues. When it finishes, if anything was
    registered, the errors are folded — each case receives the error, with `prior` (the
    `Optional[focus]` it was raised at) and `accrual` (the accumulation so far) as named
    contextual values — and the result is aborted against the enclosing `Tactic[accrual]`.
    Each case returns the new accumulation.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
    import dysasymptotics.linearSize

    case class Invalid(problems: List[Text])(using Diagnostics)
    extends Error(m"${problems.size} problems")

    recover:
      case Invalid(problems) => problems
    . protect:
        track[Pointer](Invalid(Nil)):
          case error: Number.Error =>
            val where = prior.let(_.text).or(t"?")
            Invalid(accrual.problems :+ t"$where: ${error.text}")
        . protect:
            focus(prior.or(Pointer.Self)(t"age")):
              raise(Number.Error(t"abc", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
            List()
    // List(t"age: abc")
    ```

    The focus type is chosen by the structure being traversed — a `JsonPointer` for JSON, a
    path for XML, a cell reference for a spreadsheet — so a position is reported in the
    data's own vocabulary. `validate` is the same construct returning the accumulation as a
    plain value rather than raising it.

entry .Tracking   a `track` handler, waiting for the block it protects
  detail
      `track[focus](initial) { cases }` is a `Tracking[accrual, lambda, focus]` holding the
      initial accumulation and the fold; `.protect(block)` runs the block with a fresh
      `TrackFoci` and a `TrackTactic` per covered error type, and needs a `Tactic[accrual]^`
      and a `Diagnostics` in scope. Used only as `track[…](…) { … }.protect { … }`. A function
      *value* typed `… raises E tracks F` cannot be written under capture checking, so a
      helper wrapping `track` should be an `inline def` whose body is spliced into `protect`.
  refer contingency.track
  entry .protect   runs a block, registering covered errors with their foci; aborts with the fold if any
  entry #initial   the accumulation before any error
  entry #lambda   the fold: each error, with its `prior` focus and the `accrual` so far, to a new accumulation

entry .validate   collects chosen errors with their foci into a value which is returned, not raised
    `validate[focus](initial) { cases }.protect(block)` is `track` whose result is the
    accumulation itself: the block runs with a `Foci[focus]` and a `TrackTactic` per covered
    error type, and whatever the block returns is discarded; the fold over the registered
    errors (each case with `prior` and `accrual` in scope) is the value of the whole
    expression. The accumulation need not be an error — a `validate` collecting a `Count`,
    or a list of messages, is only ever returned. Needs a `Diagnostics`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    case class Problems(texts: List[Text])

    val problems: Problems =
      validate[Pointer](Problems(Nil)):
        case error: Number.Error => Problems(accrual.texts :+ error.text)
      . protect:
          raise(Number.Error(t"a", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
          raise(Number.Error(t"b", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))

    problems.texts   // List(t"a", t"b")
    ```

    Nested validations are fully delimited: an inner `validate` keeps its own errors, and the
    outer sees none of them.

entry .Validate   a `validate` handler, waiting for the block it protects
  detail
      `validate[focus](initial) { cases }` is a `Validate[accrual, lambda, focus]`; `.protect
      (block)` runs the block with a fresh `TrackFoci` and a `TrackTactic` per covered type,
      then returns the fold. It can be constructed directly — `Validate[Tally, [r] =>> r
      raises CErrorA, Pointer](initial, cases)` — where a helper needs to name the covered
      types itself.
  refer contingency.validate
  entry .protect   runs a block, registering covered errors with their foci; returns the fold
  entry #initial   the accumulation before any error
  entry #lambda   the fold: each error, with its `prior` focus and the `accrual` so far, to a new accumulation

entry .accrual   the accumulation so far, inside a `track`, `validate` or `accrue` case
    Reads the contextual `accrual aka "accrual"` value a `track` or `validate` fold passes to
    each case: `case error: Number.Error => Invalid(accrual.problems :+ error.text)`. Outside
    such a case there is no such value in scope.

entry .focus   runs a block, and tags each error registered within it with a focus
    `focus(transform)(block)` evaluates the block, then — however it ended — supplements
    every error the `Foci[focus]` in scope registered during it with the focus `transform`
    produces. The transform receives `prior`, the `Optional[focus]` already attached (by an
    inner `focus`), so a traversal descending a structure composes positions from the inside
    out: `focus(prior.or(Pointer.Self)(t"name"))`. The inert `Foci.default` registers
    nothing, and `Foci#active` lets a hot loop skip the wrapping entirely.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    val foci = TrackFoci[Pointer]()
    given Foci[Pointer] = foci

    focus[Pointer, Unit](prior.or(Pointer.Self)(t"field")):
      foci.register(Number.Error(t"abc", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))

    foci.length   // 1
    ```

entry .Foci   the record of where errors were registered: the positions a `track` or `validate` collects
  detail
      A `Foci[focus]` is the mutable register a `TrackTactic` reports into: each error, with
      an `Optional[focus]` saying where in a structure it arose, which `focus` fills in as the
      traversal unwinds. `TrackFoci` is the recording implementation which `track` and
      `validate` install; `Foci.default` is the inert one in implicit scope everywhere else,
      so a method declared `tracks focus` can always be called, and only an enclosing
      `track` or `validate` observes the positions.
  refer contingency.track
  refer contingency.focus
  refer contingency.Pointer
  entry .default   the inert `Foci`, in implicit scope: registers nothing, reports no success
      Its `active` is `false`, its `length` is 0, `register` and `supplement` discard
      everything, `fold` returns the initial value and `success` is `false` (no error was
      recorded, but nothing was tracked either).
  entry #active   whether registered errors are kept; `false` only for `Foci.default`
      A hot decode loop checks this to skip its per-field `focus` wrapping, and the closure
      each wrap allocates, when no focus would ever be observed.
  entry #length   how many errors have been registered
  entry #success   whether tracking ran and no error was registered
  entry #register   records an error, with no focus yet
  entry #fold   folds the registered errors, each with its focus, into an accumulation
      `fold(initial)((focus, accumulation) => { case error => … })`: the lambda receives the
      focus and accumulation and returns a partial function over the error.
  entry #supplement   applies a transform to the focus of the last `count` errors registered
      How `focus` attaches a position: the transform takes the `Optional[focus]` already
      there and returns the new one.
  entry #truncate   drops every error registered after the first `length`
      How a `TrackTactic#tolerate` rolls back what its block recorded.
  entry #tainted   whether any error has been registered

entry .TrackFoci   the recording `Foci`: keeps every registered error with its focus
    The implementation `track` and `validate` install, with an `ArrayBuffer` of errors and
    one of their `Optional[focus]`es. It can be bound as a local given to observe foci
    without a `track` (`given Foci[Pointer] = TrackFoci()`).

entry .Pointer   a dotted path into a structure, as the focus of an error
  detail
      The simplest focus type: `Pointer.Self` is the root, and `Pointer.Child(parent, label)`
      one step down. `Pointer(t"a")(t"b")(t"c")` builds a path, which renders as `a.b.c`, and
      is the focus type the `validate` examples use where the structure has no pointer type
      of its own. It is `Encodable in Text` and `Communicable`, so it interpolates into a
      message as its dotted text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Pointer().text                      // t""
      Pointer(t"a")(t"b")(t"c").text      // t"a.b.c"
      ```
  entry .apply   `Pointer()` is the root; `Pointer(label)` a child of the root
  entry .encodable   renders a pointer as its dotted text
  entry .communicable   renders a pointer as a message of its dotted text
  entry #text   the labels joined with `.`; empty for the root
  entry #apply   `pointer(label)`: the child of this pointer with that label

entry .TrackTactic   the tactic `track` and `validate` inject: registers each error with the foci and continues
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` over a `boundary` label, an initial accumulation and the `Foci` to
      register into, with the `Diagnostics` it builds errors with. `record` registers the
      error and returns; `abort` registers it and breaks out of the block; `certify` breaks
      out if the foci are tainted; `tolerate` truncates the foci back to where they were
      before its block.
  entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` errors are built with
  entry #record   registers the error with the foci and returns
  entry #finish   a no-op, kept for the tactic's shape
  entry #certify   breaks out of the tracked block if any error has been registered
  entry #tainted   whether the foci hold any error
  entry #tolerate   a block's value, or `Unset` with the errors it registered dropped from the foci
  entry #abort   registers the error and breaks out of the tracked block

entry .Errors   several errors reported together, each keyed by the text of its focus
  detail
      Error 218: _`n` accrued errors: `message` at `focus`; …_. An `Error` holding
      `(Text, Error)` pairs, for a validation which collects every problem before failing.
      It is built empty and grown with `+`, and a focus's error is looked up with `apply`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val errors = Errors() + (t"age", Number.Error(t"abc", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
      errors(t"age")       // the Number.Error
      errors(t"missing")   // Unset
      ```
  entry #errors   the `(focus, error)` pairs, most recent first
  entry #+   `errors + (focus, error)`: a new `Errors` with one more pair
  entry #apply   the error recorded at a focus, or `Unset`

entry .Validation   several messages reported together, each at a `Pointer`
  detail
      Error 59: _`n` messages: `message` at `pointer`; …_ (with `no messages`, `one
      message`, `two messages`… for small counts, and a message at `Pointer.Self` shown
      without a position). An `Error` holding `(Pointer, Message)` pairs for a pipeline which
      walks a structure and accumulates what it finds wrong, rather than aborting at the
      first. Built empty and grown with `+`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val validation = Validation() + (Pointer(t"name"), m"the value was not set")
      validation(Pointer(t"name"))   // m"the value was not set"
      validation.text.text           // t"one message: the value was not set at name"
      ```
  entry .text   the message for a list of `(Pointer, Message)` pairs: a count, then each message at its pointer
  entry #messages   the pairs, most recent first
  entry #+   `validation + (pointer, message)`: a new `Validation` with one more pair
  entry #apply   the message recorded at a pointer, or `Unset`
  entry #text   the validation's message, as `Validation.text` renders it

entry .Expectation   the home of `Expectation.Error`, raised when `capture`'s block succeeds
  detail
      `capture` expects its block to fail and returns the error it raised; `Expectation`
      names that expectation so its disappointment has somewhere to live.
  refer contingency.capture
  entry .Error   raised by `capture` when the block was expected to fail but succeeded
    detail
        Error 841: _the expression was expected to fail, but succeeded_. Carries the value the
        block produced instead, so a test can see what was returned. `capture` needs a
        `Tactic[Expectation.Error[success]]` in scope for it, which `strategies.throwUnsafely`
        supplies.
    entry #result   the value the block produced instead of failing

entry .venture   evaluates a block eagerly, remembering whether it recorded any error
    `venture(block)` runs the block at once, under a `VentureTactic` interposed before the
    ambient `Tactic[error]`: every error it raises is recorded into the ambient scope exactly
    as it would be outside, but the result is a `Venture[value]` — the value if the block
    recorded nothing, or the `Failed` marker if it did. An `abort` inside a venture is
    *delimited*: it records its error and abandons only the venture, so sibling ventures each
    contribute their full error sets, and code which later forces a failed venture with
    `venture()` is skipped rather than fed a placeholder.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    def person(name: Text, age: Text): Person raises Number.Error =
      val checkedName = venture:
        if name == t"" then abort(Number.Error(name, Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))
        else name

      val checkedAge = venture(age.as[Int])

      venture:
        if checkedName().length > checkedAge()
        then abort(Number.Error(age, Int, Number.Error.Reason.OutOfRange))

      guard:
        Person(checkedName(), checkedAge())
    ```

    Under a fail-fast ambient tactic (`safely`, `throwUnsafely`, `attempt`…) the forwarded
    `record` escapes immediately, and `venture` is transparent eager evaluation: the same
    body serves every strategy. The block receives one capability which is both its tactic
    and its `Guard` (a `Venturer[error]`), so a venture may be forced inside another.

entry .guard   runs a block only if nothing has been recorded so far; otherwise surrenders the scope
    `guard(block)` checks the ambient `Tactic[error]`'s `tainted`: if clean, the block runs
    with a `Guard` capability in scope (so ventures can be forced within it); if tainted, the
    block is skipped by certifying the tactic, which surrenders the whole aggregation scope to
    its accumulated errors. Forcing a failed `Venture` inside the block escapes the same way.
    Under a fail-fast tactic `tainted` is always `false`, so `guard` is the identity.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

    safely[Number.Error]:
      val n = venture(t"abc".as[Int])
      guard(n() * 2)
    // Unset: under `safely` the failing venture escapes before any guard
    ```

entry .Guard   the witness of an enclosing skip-scope, which a failed `Venture` can escape to
  detail
      A capability provided to the block of a `venture` or `guard`: forcing a `Venture` with
      `venture()` requires one, so that forcing is only possible where a lexical region exists
      to abandon. `escape()` performs the abandonment — for a `venture`, failing the venture
      itself; for a `guard`, certifying the ambient tactic. With no skip-scope in context,
      forcing does not compile: there would be nowhere well-defined to skip to.
  refer contingency.venture
  refer contingency.guard
  entry #escape   abandons the enclosing skip-scope; does not return

entry .Venturer   the one capability a `venture` block receives: its tactic, which is also its `Guard`
    `Venturer[error]` is `Tactic[error] & Guard`: the venture's delimiting tactic and its own
    skip-scope witness are the same object, passed once, since the same capability cannot be
    passed twice under separation checking. A named alias because a parenthesised capturing
    intersection cannot be parsed in context-function position.

entry .Venture   the outcome of a `venture`: the value, or the marker that errors were recorded
  detail
      `Venture[value]` is an opaque union of `value` and `Venture.Failed`, so a successful
      venture allocates nothing — opaque, unlike `Optional`, so that `apply()` resolves only
      on ventures. The errors themselves are not held here: they were recorded into the
      ambient tactic at the venture's declaration, and `Failed` only marks the value unusable,
      which is why forcing a failed venture reports nothing further.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      safely[Number.Error]:
        val n: Venture[Int] = venture(t"42".as[Int])
        n.ready   // true
        guard(n() + 1)
      // 43
      ```
  refer contingency.venture
  refer contingency.guard
  refer contingency.Guard
  entry .Failed   the marker of a venture which recorded errors
  entry .Type   the opaque type `Failed.type | value` which `Venture[value]` aliases
  entry .apply   `Venture(value)` is a ready venture; `venture()` forces one, escaping if it failed
      `Venture.apply(value)` wraps a value as a ready venture. The extension `venture()` on an
      instance is the value, or escapes to the enclosing skip-scope if the venture failed: it
      needs a `Guard^` in scope — an enclosing `venture` or `guard` block — and does not
      compile without one.
  entry .failed   a failed venture, of any value type
  entry .ready   whether the venture holds a value rather than `Failed`
  entry .vouch   the value, panicking if the venture failed
      Escape-free forcing for a venture the caller has already checked with `ready` — a
      decoder which checks a whole batch before using any. Reaching a failed venture here is
      a logic error, so it panics rather than skipping.

entry .VentureTactic   the tactic `venture` interposes: records into the ambient scope, aborts only the venture
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` and `Guard` over the ambient tactic and the venture's `boundary`
      label. `record` counts the error and forwards it to the ambient tactic; `abort` does the
      same and breaks out of the venture with `Venture.failed`; `certify` fails the venture if
      it has recorded anything; `tolerate` delegates to the ambient tactic's `tolerate` and
      restores the venture's own count; `escape` fails the venture. Under a fail-fast ambient
      tactic the forwarded `record` never returns.
  entry #diagnostics   the ambient tactic's `Diagnostics`
  entry #failed   whether an error was recorded during this venture's evaluation
      Distinct from `tainted`, which also reflects the ambient scope: an earlier venture's
      failure must not fail this one.
  entry #tainted   whether this venture, or the ambient scope, has recorded an error
  entry #record   counts the error and forwards it to the ambient tactic
  entry #abort   counts and forwards the error, then abandons the venture
  entry #certify   abandons the venture if it has recorded anything
  entry #tolerate   the ambient tactic's `tolerate`, with this venture's count restored afterwards
  entry #escape   abandons the venture, recording nothing further

entry .ThrowTactic   the tactic which throws: behind `throwUnsafely`, `throwSafely`, `unsafely` and `throwErrors`
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` whose `record` and `abort` both throw the error as an unchecked
      exception, built with `Diagnostics.capture` so it carries a stack trace. A throwing
      tactic captures nothing (`CanThrow` is erased), which makes it the natural ambient
      strategy: `.ambient` is the pure instance `strategies.throwUnsafely` provides, and the
      scoped forms mint their own. Contravariant in `error`, so one `ThrowTactic[Hazard]`
      satisfies every `Tactic[error]`. A local `given tactic: Tactic[Json.Error] = ThrowTactic()`
      is the way to supply a throwing tactic to one method without importing a strategy.
  refer contingency.strategies
  entry .ambient   the pure `ThrowTactic[Hazard]^{}` behind `strategies.throwUnsafely`
      A pure value, as `unsafeExceptions.canThrowAny` is: an unscoped throw retains no return
      point, and a tracked ambient tactic could satisfy neither a derivation's synthesised
      thunk nor a lambda which raises through it.
  entry #diagnostics   `Diagnostics.capture`: a thrown error carries its stack trace
  entry #record   throws the error
  entry #abort   throws the error
  entry #certify   a no-op: a thrown error has already escaped

entry .OptionalTactic   the tactic `safely` and `dare` install: breaks out with `Unset`
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` over a `boundary.Label[Optional[success]]`: `record` and `abort` both
      break out of the block with `Unset`, and the error is never materialised, so it is built
      with `Diagnostics.omit`.
  refer contingency.safely
  entry #Result   `Optional[success]`
  entry #Return   `Optional[success]`
  entry #diagnostics   `Diagnostics.omit`: the error is discarded, so no trace is captured
  entry #record   breaks out of the block with `Unset`
  entry #abort   breaks out of the block with `Unset`
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .AttemptTactic   the tactic `attempt` installs: breaks out with `Attempt.Failure(error)`
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` over a `boundary.Label[Attempt[success, error]]`, with the
      `Diagnostics` in scope at the `attempt`: `record` and `abort` break out with the error
      wrapped as a `Failure`.
  refer contingency.attempt
  entry #Result   `Attempt[success, error]`
  entry #Return   `Attempt[success, error]`
  entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` given at the `attempt`
  entry #record   breaks out with `Attempt.Failure(error)`
  entry #abort   breaks out with `Attempt.Failure(error)`
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .EitherTactic   the tactic `capture` installs: breaks out with `Left(error)`
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` over a `boundary.Label[Either[error, success]]`, with the
      `Diagnostics` in scope: `record` and `abort` break out with `Left(error)`. `capture`
      unwraps the `Either` to return the error, or aborts with `Expectation.Error` on a
      `Right`.
  refer contingency.capture
  entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` given at the `capture`
  entry #record   breaks out with `Left(error)`
  entry #abort   breaks out with `Left(error)`
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .AmalgamateTactic   the tactic `amalgamate` installs: breaks out with the error itself
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` over a `boundary.Label[success | error]`, with the `Diagnostics` in
      scope: `record` and `abort` break out with the bare error, which the caller
      distinguishes from a value by a type match.
  refer contingency.amalgamate
  entry #Result   `success | error`
  entry #Return   `success | error`
  entry #diagnostics   the `Diagnostics` given at the `amalgamate`
  entry #record   breaks out with the error
  entry #abort   breaks out with the error
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .HaltTactic   a tactic for macros: an error halts the compilation with its labelled message
  detail
      An `open class` taking `Quotes`, for `error <: Error`: `record` and `abort` both call
      `halt` with the error's `labelled` message (its `SN-` code and text), so a parse which
      fails at expansion time is reported as a compile error. `abortive` installs one; a
      macro which wants the error positioned at a particular tree subclasses it and overrides
      `record`/`abort` to pass the position.
  refer contingency.abortive
  entry #diagnostics   `Diagnostics.omit`, as a given: a compile-time error needs no stack trace
  entry #record   halts the compilation with the error's message
  entry #abort   halts the compilation with the error's message
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .UncheckedTactic   the tactic which throws an error classified `Unchecked`
  detail
      A `Tactic[error]` whose `record` and `abort` throw, built with
      `errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics`. The `error is Unchecked` evidence is checked
      at the summon site by `strategies.uncheckedErrors`, which provides the pure
      `.ambient` instance, rather than held by the tactic.
  refer contingency.Unchecked
  entry .ambient   the pure `UncheckedTactic[Hazard]^{}` behind `strategies.uncheckedErrors`
  entry #diagnostics   stack traces are captured, as a given
  entry #record   throws the error
  entry #abort   throws the error
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .FatalTactic   the tactic which terminates the process with the error's `Exit` status
  detail
      A `Tactic[exception]` for an error type with an `is Fatal` given: `record` and `abort`
      both ask the `Fatal` instance for the error's `Exit` status and `terminate()` the
      process with it. `strategies.fatalErrors` provides the pure instance. Not exported to
      the `soundness` umbrella: it is reached through the strategy, not named directly.
  refer contingency.Fatal
  entry #diagnostics   stack traces are captured, as a given
  entry #record   terminates the process with the error's status
  entry #abort   terminates the process with the error's status
  entry #certify   a no-op

entry .Unchecked   the marker typeclass for an error which may be thrown without being handled
    `error is Unchecked` has no members, so the given can be `erased`: `erased given
    AsciiError is Unchecked`. With `strategies.uncheckedErrors` imported, a method raising an
    unchecked error can be called with no tactic for it in scope, and the error is thrown.
    The classification is the author's assertion that the failure is not worth handling at
    each site, while still declared in the type.

entry .Fatal   the typeclass for an error which ends the process, saying with what status
  detail
      `error is Fatal` maps an error to the `Exit` status the process should end with; under
      `strategies.fatalErrors`, raising such an error terminates the process. It suits
      failures during initialisation, where there is no meaningful way to continue.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class InitError(reason: Text)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"initialization failed: $reason")

      given InitError is Fatal = error => Exit.Fail(127)
      ```
  refer contingency.strategies
  entry #status   the `Exit` status for an error
  entry #contramap   a `Fatal` for another type, by mapping it to this one

entry .Mitigable   the typeclass saying how one error type becomes another
  detail
      `error2 is Mitigable to error` (`error mitigates error2`, in the infix spelling) has one
      method, `mitigate`, translating an `error2` into an `error`. With
      `strategies.mitigation` imported, any `Tactic[error]` in scope automatically yields a
      `Tactic[error2]`, so a low-level failure is translated at the boundary of the API which
      declares the outer error, without each call site restating it. `mitigate { cases }`
      is the explicit, block-scoped form.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      case class InputError(text: Text)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"the input $text is not acceptable")

      given Number.Error is Mitigable to InputError = error => InputError(error.text)
      ```

      The translation runs outside any `Diagnostics` of the raising site's; import one, or
      build the new error `(using error.diagnostics)`.
  refer contingency.strategies
  refer contingency.mitigate
  entry #Self   the error type translated from
  entry #Result   the error type translated to
  entry #mitigate   the translation of one error into the other

entry .lest   `optional.lest(error)`: the value, or aborts with the error if `Unset`
    An extension on `Optional[value]`: `optional.lest(error)` is the value when present, and
    `abort(error)` against the `Tactic[error]^` in scope when absent. It turns an absence into
    a declared failure at the point where the value is required.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val port: Optional[Int] = Unset
    port.lest(Number.Error(t"", Int, Number.Error.Reason.Unparseable))   // throws
    ```

entry .dare   `optional.dare(block)`: applies a fallible block to a present value, absent on failure
    An extension on `Optional[value]`: `optional.dare[error](value => …)` is `Unset` if the
    optional is absent, else the block's result, or `Unset` if the block raises — `let`
    composed with `safely`, with the same `OptionalTactic` and no stack trace captured.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val text: Optional[Text] = t"42"
    text.dare[Number.Error](_.as[Int])   // 42
    ```

entry .survive   the results of a fallible lambda over a collection, with the failures dropped
    An extension on any `Iterable[element]`, and on any receiver which is `Traversable by
    element`: `collection.survive[error](element => …)` applies the lambda to each element
    under `safely`, and keeps only the results which did not raise. The `Iterable` form
    returns an `Iterable[result]`; the `Traversable` form materialises a `List[result]`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    List(t"1", t"x", t"3").survive[Number.Error](_.as[Int])   // List(1, 3)
    ```

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `recover`, `mitigate`, `accrue`, `handle`, `track` and `validate`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The object reads a handler's match cases at compile time to find
      which error types they cover, builds the type lambda `[result] =>> (Tactic[E1], …,
      Tactic[En]) ?=> result` which the handler values carry, and splices the tactics into a
      `protect`ed block. The patterns it accepts: a typed pattern (a union covers each
      member), a binding, alternatives, or an exhaustive case-class extractor; a wildcard or a
      non-exhaustive pattern is a compile error.
  entry .unwrap   strips `Inlined` and single-definition blocks from a term
  entry .unpack   the members of a union type, dealiased, as a list
  entry .track   builds a `Tracking` from a `track` handler
  entry .validate   builds a `Validate` from a `validate` handler
  entry .trackWithin   the body of `Tracking.protect`
  entry .validateWithin   the body of `Validate.protect`
  entry .recoverBuild   builds a `Recovery` from a `recover` handler
  entry .mitigateBuild   builds a `Mitigation` from a `mitigate` handler
  entry .accrueBuild   builds an `Accrual` from an `accrue` handler
  entry .handleBuild   builds a `Handler` from a `handle` handler
  entry .protectBody   the body of `Handler.protect`
  entry .mitigateBody   the body of `Mitigation.protect`
  entry .recoverBody   the body of `Recovery.protect`
  entry .accrueBody   the body of `Accrual.protect`
